{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "comment": "Keyring authority rotation. Each case starts from an approved source whose authority came from an initial keyring (initialTrusted/initialRevoked, signed by the reviewed pin), optionally applies an accepted intermediate keyring (via: version, trusted, revoked, signedBy, dbSignedBy), then republishes the keyring. PIN, ROT and OLD stand for the fingerprints below; ARCH stands for an unrelated global signing key (an Arch/CachyOS or administrator key). pacman-key --populate applies the revoked list to the shared keyring, so a revocation may name only a key this source has actually used: the pin, an earlier revocation, or an observed signer (a primary whose VALIDSIG verified this source's database or keyring archive). Only signers observed before an approval count: a signer cannot revoke itself in the archive that introduces it. A fingerprint merely listed in a trusted list, now or in an earlier archive, is never revocable. signedBy: pinned (PIN), rotated (ROT), old (OLD). dbSignedBy (default pinned) signs the final database. A trusted entry may carry an ownertrust column (NAME:VALUE); haseen never imports it.",
